# saya runner child sandbox — Linux (Landlock + unprivileged namespaces) DRAFT

> **Status: draft, UNVERIFIED.** No Linux behaviour in this document was
> observed. The M5-3 spike host is macOS 26.6.2; the Linux probe
> (`crates/saya-harness/tests/sandbox_probe.rs`) has not yet run on any Linux
> machine, so every factual claim below about Linux is a citation of the
> kernel's own documentation — marked **[UNVERIFIED]** — or an unmeasured
> plan. Per the spike's rule, an unmeasured claim is worse than a gap: nothing
> here is a decision, and the `landlock` dependency has deliberately **not**
> been added. The implementation slice must measure every **[UNVERIFIED]**
> item on a real Linux host before this draft becomes a profile.

Source policy: `RunSandbox { fs_roots: [run dir, child temp], net_allow: [(host, port)] }`
Probe: `crates/saya-harness/tests/sandbox_probe.rs` (records Landlock ABI and
user+network namespace availability; records `landlock_enforcement` as failed
until the `landlock` dependency lands, so Linux cannot be "proven" yet —
fail closed).

## 1. Measured facts on this host

None. Linux was not measured. The probe exists and will measure:

- `landlock_abi` — raw `landlock_create_ruleset(NULL, 0,
  LANDLOCK_CREATE_RULESET_VERSION)` version query (syscall 444 via
  `libc::SYS_landlock_create_ruleset`; the locked `libc` 0.2.189 carries the
  constant for gnu/musl x86_64 and arm64).
- `user_network_namespace_unprivileged` — `fork`, then `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)`
  then `unshare(CLONE_NEWNET)` in the child (forked because
  `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)` is refused in a multithreaded process and the test
  harness is threaded **[UNVERIFIED — per unshare(2)]**), reporting errno over a
  pipe.
- informational: `/sys/kernel/security/lsm` (is `landlock` in the LSM list),
  `/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces`.

## 2. Landlock ruleset plan (filesystem confinement)

Shape mirrors the macOS draft, deny by default by construction:

1. Create a ruleset with `handled_access_fs` = every `LANDLOCK_ACCESS_FS_*`
   right the kernel ABI supports: `EXECUTE`, `READ_FILE`, `READ_DIR`,
   `REMOVE_FILE`, `REMOVE_DIR`, `MAKE_CHAR`, `MAKE_DIR`, `MAKE_REG`,
   `MAKE_SOCK`, `MAKE_FIFO`, `MAKE_BLOCK`, `MAKE_SYM`, plus (ABI ≥ 3)
   `REFER`, (ABI ≥ 5) `TRUNCATE` **[UNVERIFIED — list and semantics per
   `Documentation/userspace-api/landlock.rst`]**. Handling fewer rights than
   the kernel supports would silently unrestrict — refuse to run if the
   probe's ABI and the ruleset's handled set disagree.
2. `PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS` on the child before restriction **[UNVERIFIED —
   required by landlock(7) for unprivileged use]**.
3. One `LANDLOCK_RULE_PATH_BENEATH` rule per `fs_roots` entry
   (`{RUN_DIR}`, `{CHILD_TEMP}`): allowed_access = READ_FILE | READ_DIR |
   WRITE_FILE | REMOVE_FILE | REMOVE_DIR | MAKE_* | TRUNCATE **[UNVERIFIED
   set]**. Everything outside is denied by the ruleset itself — there is no
   allowlist to forget.
4. `restrict_self()`; the enforced domain is then unpriviligable.

The enforcement probe (canary child: read/write inside roots must succeed,
write outside must fail with EACCES/EPERM, exec outside the roots denied) is
the entry condition for registering the runner on Linux — same deny-evidence
discipline as the macOS probe, with the `landlock` crate
(see §5) doing the syscall marshalling.

## 3. Namespace plan (process + egress isolation)

Order matters **[UNVERIFIED — per user_namespaces(7)]**:

1. `fork` (the runner is multithreaded; `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)` is refused
   in a multithreaded process — the plan's runner spawns the child from a
   dedicated pre-exec step, which must be single-threaded at that moment, or
   the probe's fork-based shape is used directly).
2. Child: `unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER)` — unprivileged, if
   `user.max_user_namespaces > 0` and the distribution has not disabled
   unprivileged user namespaces (Debian's `kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone`,
   container runtimes' seccomp profiles). All failures must surface as the
   probe's recorded `user_network_namespace_unprivileged` failure, not a
   fallback.
3. Child: write `deny` to `/proc/self/setgroups`, then map the run uid/gid
   into the new namespace via `/proc/self/uid_map` and `/proc/self/gid_map`
   **[UNVERIFIED — per user_namespaces(7); the exact map line for an
   unprivileged single-uid mapping is unmeasured]**.
4. Child: `unshare(CLONE_NEWNET)`. Inside the new netns there are **no**
   network interfaces — not even loopback carries parent traffic. This is
   the egress denial mechanism.
5. The runner program then executes under: Landlock ruleset (filesystem) +
   fresh netns (network) + no-new-privs.

## 4. net_allow on Linux — the open decision

Three measured-by-documentation facts collide **[UNVERIFIED]**:

- A fresh netns has **no** network at all: every child is fully egress-denied,
  which cannot honour a non-empty `net_allow`.
- Landlock's network support (ABI ≥ 4, i.e. kernel ≥ 6.7) restricts TCP
  `bind`/`connect` **by port only** — there is no host/IP dimension in
  `LANDLOCK_RULE_NET_PORT` **[UNVERIFIED]**. So Landlock alone can enforce
  `(*, port)`, not `(host, port)`.
- A selective-egress design therefore needs either (a) `net_allow = []` plus
  netns (deny everything), or (b) no netns plus Landlock port rules (port
  exact, host wildcard — same shape Seatbelt's `"*"` gives), or (c) a
  parent- or in-sandbox proxy that owns the allowlist — a new, itself
  unmeasured network component.

The spike could not choose between these from this host. The implementation
slice must measure the Landlock ABI reality on the actual target
distributions first (Ubuntu LTS and Debian stable ship kernels well below
6.7 **[UNVERIFIED — distribution kernels were not measured]**), then record
the choice in an amendment, with the security reviewer's sign-off below.

## 5. Fail-closed mapping

`sandbox_probe.rs` computes the verdict; the runner consumes it:

- required checks on Linux: `landlock_abi`, `user_network_namespace_unprivileged`,
  `landlock_enforcement`;
- `landlock_enforcement` is recorded **failed** until the `landlock`-based
  canary probe exists — so Linux fails closed today by construction;
- any required check failing on any host ⇒ runner tool not registered, plans
  requesting the runner scope refused. No warning mode, no degraded mode.

## 6. Kernel ABI reference (unverified, for the implementation slice)

**[UNVERIFIED — from the kernel's landlock documentation, not observed
here]**: ABI 1 (Linux 5.13) filesystem rights; ABI 2 (5.19) symlink handling;
ABI 3 (6.2) `TRUNCATE`; ABI 4 (6.7) TCP bind/connect; ABI 5 (6.12) scoped
signals; ABI 6 (6.12) scoped Unix sockets. The probe records the version
number the running kernel reports; the runner gates on what it measured, not
on this table.